# ── Visual style presets ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# GENERATED by `python3 docs/design/palette_gen.py --emit-presets`. Do not edit
# a hex here; change the theme in `docs/studiosaas/palette.py` and re-emit.
# `backend/tests/test_theme_layering.py` fails on any drift.
#
# EIGHT NAMED THEMES, plus a free "custom" knob. There were eight until
# 2026-08-06, when they were collapsed to one because the PAPER carried the
# industry hue and whichever semantic role shared that hue stopped being
# visible — five of the seven light themes had one. That fix was real and it
# is still here (see `CHROMA_FLOOR` / `CHROMA_FLOOR_NEAR` / the chroma-derived
# neutral ramp in `palette.py`), but removing the eight identities was not
# required to ship it, and an owner opening Studio Admin the next day reported
# exactly that loss: "选择颜色主题" over a single empty swatch, with the
# right-hand preview showing the same fixed description for every industry.
#
# 2026-08-06 (second pass) restores the eight, with the actual defect fixed at
# its actual cause — every one of the 1080 assertions across these nine
# theme-modes passes with the ORIGINAL hues from before the collapse, because
# the generator itself is what got stronger, not the input it was given.
#
# Industry no longer AUTO-APPLIES a theme. `product-home.html:651` says
# "Templates change the vocabulary and the forms, not just the colours" — an
# industry still RECOMMENDS one (`INDUSTRY_STYLE_RECOMMENDATIONS` below, shown
# as a badge in the picker), but Studio Admin no longer writes that theme into
# a draft just because an industry card was clicked. That wiring, not the
# eight-theme system, was the actual defect.
#
# "custom" is the ninth entry: a studio whose brand hue matches none of the
# eight, usually because it has a logo. Only its HUE is taken from the input —
# lightness and saturation stay solved — so a fluorescent logo becomes a
# proper deep colour rather than a button nobody can read. See
# `accent_hue_from` in palette.py and `Design_Constraints.md` section 1.4.
#
#     python3 docs/design/palette_gen.py            # verify
#     python3 docs/design/palette_gen.py --table    # inspect

